    explanation: "A network latency calculator estimates the propagation delay, the time a signal takes to travel a given distance through a transmission medium such as fibre-optic cable, copper, or open air. This is the floor on latency set by physics: no amount of bandwidth or optimisation can beat the speed at which a signal physically crosses the distance, which is why geography fundamentally limits how fast two points can communicate.\n\nUnderstanding this delay matters for anyone designing distributed systems, choosing data-centre locations, or troubleshooting slow connections. It explains why a server across the world always feels less responsive than one nearby, why content delivery networks place servers close to users, and why real-time applications like gaming and video calls are so sensitive to distance. Separating the physics-imposed minimum from other delays helps you know when latency can be improved and when it cannot.\n\nThe calculation works because signals in cable travel slower than light in a vacuum by a factor related to the material's refractive index, roughly 1.47 for fibre and about 1.5 for copper. Dividing the vacuum speed of light by that factor gives the effective speed, and distance divided by effective speed gives the one-way time, doubled for the round trip. Real latency adds routing, switching, queuing, and processing delays on top, so this is the theoretical best case. Network architects and engineers use a latency calculator to reason about the geographic limits of performance.",
